On 2011-11-04 04:59, Howard S Shubs wrote:
> In article<j8mjnq$mjb$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>,
>   Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>  wrote:
>
>> Huh? I'm not sure I understand. I don't see how runoff ties in here. An
>> LN03 can be sent normal text, and it will print that text literally,
>> with the chosen font. It also understand various escape sequences, which
>> you can use to change fonts, change attributes (pretty much similar to a
>> VT100), and also to do sixel graphics.
>
> RUNOFF understands how to make an LNI file, which can produce
> Postscript-like results.  I've not looked at what's required to produce
> an LNI file by hand, but I suspect it wouldn't be a fun task.

I'm not sure what the format is of an LNI file, but from the printer 
point of view, we are then talking about LNI files, which really don't 
require RUNOFF at all.

RUNOFF can be told to generate various different control sequences to 
accomplish things like underlines, bold, and so on. But that just cause 
RUNOFF to generate an output file with various characters in it.
If you ask RUNOFF to write out something that is working on an LN03, it 
will do escape sequences to accomplish this.
But the scape sequences are no secret or magic. They are pretty well 
documented.

If you have a really tricky RUNOFF, it could start playing graphically, 
at which point the output for a LN03 would need to be sixel graphics. 
That is still done using escape sequences, but you no longer output the 
text straight as such, but instead output a bitmap.

It's however all very different from what a postscript printer would 
understand, and I seriously doubt any postscript printer could 
understand escape sequences without breaking the postscript language.

So, I don't see how any output intended for an LN03 could ever work on a 
postscript printer. But, if you have some software in between, it could 
detect the format of the output, and convert it to the correct form for 
the actual printer. I think, for instance, that Printserver (or what the 
name is for that software in VMS) will do this for you.
It is also pretty common in Unix systems.

	Johnny

