In article <j921pj$p91$1@Iltempo.Update.UU.SE>, Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> writes:

> But that means RUNOFF would produce postscript output, since he claimed 
> that he just ran RUNOFF, saying that he wanted LN01 output, got an LNI 
> file, and printed that on the printer.

   No, RUNOFF did not generate PostScript.  As far as I recall, RUNOFF
   never did know PostScript.

> This would then imply that a LNI

   No, .LNI is the extension used when you tell RUNOFF to generate LN01
   compatable output by the qualifier /DEVICE=LN01 .
 
> Or else there is indeed some additional filter in 
> between, which understands the LNI file (whatever format that is), and 
> prepends some postscript code to the output, for things to work.

   The "filter" is the VMS print symbiont for PostScript printers, which
   can take plain text and wrap it up in the necessary PostScript code
   to print text and do page changes.  It did not translate LN01
   sequences to PostScript commands, it just left them in the "plain
   text" portion of the PostScript stream.  The LN03-R ScriptPrinter
   understood them.



